      "text_raw": "Such a current mirror is\nshown in this slide. Its cur-\nrent gain A is now B, which\nI\nis the ratio between W /L\n2 2\nand W /L .\n1 1\nThe simplest current\nmirror has a short between\nthe Drain and Gate of tran-\nsistor M1. The specifications\ncan be improved a lot by\ninsertion of an opamp with\nhigh gain A . This circuit is\n0\nonly one of the many pos-\nsible configurations of cur-\nrent mirrors, as shown in\nChapter 3.\nThis one has the advantage that it can operate at low supply voltages. Indeed the Drain of\ntransistor M1 is maintained at 0.2 V rather than at 0.9 V for a simple current mirror.\nThe loop gain LG is very large. The input resistance is therefore very small.\nThe output resistance, however, is not so large. It is merely the output resistance r of\no2\ntransistor M2.",
